Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether, given $f \in L_1([a,b])$ with $f(x) \to +\infty$ as $x \to x_0$, there always exists $g \in L_1([a,b])$ with $f = o(g)$ at $x_0$.

## Proof

The answer is **yes**. We construct such a $g$ explicitly.

**Setup.** Since $f(x) \to +\infty$ as $x \to x_0$, there exists $\delta_0 > 0$ (with $(x_0-\delta_0,\, x_0+\delta_0)\cap[a,b]$ nontrivial on each side where applicable) such that $f(x) \geq 1$ for all $x$ with $0 < |x-x_0| < \delta_0$, $x \in [a,b]$. In particular $f > 0$ near $x_0$.

**Right side construction** (if $x_0 < b$). For $t \in (0,\delta_0]$ define
$$F_+(t) \;=\; \int_{x_0}^{x_0+t} f(x)\,dx.$$
Then $F_+(0)=0$, $F_+(t) \geq t > 0$ for $t \in (0,\delta_0]$, and $F_+$ is absolutely continuous with $F_+'(t) = f(x_0+t)$ a.e. Define
$$h_+(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{\sqrt{F_+(x-x_0)}}, \qquad x \in (x_0,\, x_0+\delta_0].$$

**Integrability on the right.** For any $\varepsilon \in (0,\delta_0]$, since $F_+ \geq \varepsilon$ is not needed — rather $F_+(t) \geq t > 0$ on $[\varepsilon, \delta_0]$, so $u \mapsto 2\sqrt{u}$ is $C^1$ on the range of $F_+|_{[\varepsilon,\delta_0]}$, and by the chain rule for absolutely continuous functions:
$$\int_{\varepsilon}^{\delta_0} \frac{F_+'(t)}{\sqrt{F_+(t)}}\,dt \;=\; 2\sqrt{F_+(t)}\Big|_{\varepsilon}^{\delta_0} \;=\; 2\sqrt{F_+(\delta_0)} - 2\sqrt{F_+(\varepsilon)}.$$
Letting $\varepsilon \to 0^+$ and using $F_+(\varepsilon) \to 0$:
$$\int_{x_0}^{x_0+\delta_0} f(x)\,h_+(x)\,dx \;=\; \int_{0}^{\delta_0} \frac{f(x_0+t)}{\sqrt{F_+(t)}}\,dt \;=\; 2\sqrt{F_+(\delta_0)} \;<\; \infty.$$

**Left side** (if $x_0 > a$). Symmetrically set $F_-(t) = \int_{x_0-t}^{x_0} f(x)\,dx$ and $h_-(x) = 1/\sqrt{F_-(x_0-x)}$ for $x \in [x_0-\delta_0, x_0)$. The identical argument gives
$$\int_{x_0-\delta_0}^{x_0} f(x)\,h_-(x)\,dx \;=\; 2\sqrt{F_-(\delta_0)} \;<\; \infty.$$

**Definition of $g$.** Set
$$g(x) = \begin{cases} f(x)\,h_+(x), & x \in (x_0,\, x_0+\delta_0]\cap[a,b],\\ f(x)\,h_-(x), & x \in [x_0-\delta_0, x_0)\cap[a,b],\\ f(x), & x \notin (x_0-\delta_0,\, x_0+\delta_0),\\ 0, & x = x_0. \end{cases}$$

**$g \in L_1([a,b])$.** Away from $x_0$, $g = f \in L_1$. Near $x_0$, the two integrals computed above are finite, so
$$\int_a^b |g(x)|\,dx \;\leq\; 2\sqrt{F_+(\delta_0)} + 2\sqrt{F_-(\delta_0)} + \int_{[a,b]\setminus(x_0-\delta_0,x_0+\delta_0)} |f(x)|\,dx \;<\; \infty.$$

**$f = o(g)$ at $x_0$.** For $x \to x_0^+$ (the left side is identical):
$$\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} \;=\; \frac{1}{h_+(x)} \;=\; \sqrt{F_+(x-x_0)} \;\xrightarrow{x\to x_0^+}\; \sqrt{F_+(0)} \;=\; 0,$$
since $F_+$ is continuous and $F_+(0)=0$. Thus $f(x)/g(x) \to 0$, i.e., $f = o(g)$ as $x \to x_0$.

**Conclusion.** For every $f \in L_1([a,b])$ with $f(x)\to+\infty$ at $x_0$, the function $g(x) = f(x)/\sqrt{F(|x-x_0|)}$ (where $F$ is the local accumulated integral of $f$ toward $x_0$) belongs to $L_1([a,b])$ and satisfies $f = o(g)$ as $x \to x_0$. The key mechanism is that the substitution $u = F(t)$ converts $\int F'/\sqrt{F}\,dt$ into $2\sqrt{F}$, which stays bounded precisely because $F\to 0$. $\blacksquare$

